WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.802
-Do I call you "James"
or do I call you "Jimmy"?

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:03.837
-Ah, "Jimmy."
-I do?

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.204
-Yeah.
-You go by Jimmy?

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:06.272
-I go by Jimmy.
-Yeah.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.007
But you -- professionally
"James"?

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:10.810
-Yeah, it's, it's -- yeah
I'm professionally James,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.012
but everyone calls me Jimmy.
All my friends call me Jimmy.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.849
-Oh, good.
-I made this crazy decision

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.885
when I was like, 22, the first
big show I did as an actor,

00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:21.387
the producer came up to me
and she was like,

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:22.988
"You know what?"
I was like "What?"

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:25.524
she was like, "I think you
should go by "James"

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.527
And I was like, "I don't know,
everyone calls me Jimmy,

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:28.894
that might be weird and
confusing,"

00:00:28.894 --> 00:00:31.064
and she goes, "Yeah, but
James Stewart

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.165
is what Jimmy Stewart
was billed as."

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:34.968
And I said, "Alright,
well if it's good enough for

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.436
Jimmy Stewart,
it's good enough for me."

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:40.773
And so then became the most
confusing 10 years of my life.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.509
Because what happens is,
I'll go onto a set

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:44.844
and they'll go, "So what should
we call you?"

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.245
And I'll say, "Well, call me
'Jimmy.'"

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:48.014
But I don't get to everyone at
the same time.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:51.851
So I'm telling certain people
Jimmy, certain people James.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:52.952
-Oh, then people think you don't
like them.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.054
-People think I'm not a nice
person.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:56.156
-Yeah, they go
"Hey, what's this?

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.957
I'm not in the 'Jimmy' club."
-Yeah.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.492
Why does he get to
call him Jimmy

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.893
and I have to call him James?
-Yeah.

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:02.695
You actually have to start
saying your middle name as well.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:06.099
-Yeah, so you were smart.
-That's really rude, yeah.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:09.335
I got to assume you get
recognized a lot,

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.503
because I know you from a lot of
big things.

00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:14.974
"Mad Men" you were great in.
"Watchmen," "Billions."

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.210
-Thank you.
-Do you get recognized a lot?

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.546
-Yeah. I mean, it's like.
I'm not -- Not like you.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:21.447
It's not -- you know,
there's no --

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:23.782
-"Yo, James!"
-Yeah, right.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:26.486
[ Light laughter ]
I kind of get like the look,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.022
like, "Is that my cousin?
Why do I know that guy?"

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:30.523
[ Laughter ]
Or like, "Was he at

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:31.891
Thanksgiving? Was he at my
aunt's Thanksgiving?"

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.826
[ Laughter ]
-Was he at Thanksgiving?

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:36.662
-"Why is he smiling back at me?
What's wrong with that guy?"

00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:39.999
But what happens is like we --
so we have these neighbors

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.902
who are -- they're great.
There's like 13, 14 --

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.170
there's like 20 of them.
And they're really nice.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.206
And they walk our dog.

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:48.741
And during the pandemic they
were great.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.744
They'd come and walk the dog --
we have two small kids at home.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:54.314
And we didn't want the dog to be
neglected. So we had them come.

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.282
And I always noticed they'd kind
of look at me.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.217
And they'd kind of smile,
and I go,

00:01:58.217 --> 00:01:59.685
"Oh, they definitely
know my work."

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:03.890
I talk -- they must have seen
whatever show it was.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.526
And then one day, one of the
guys comes up and he goes,

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.394
"We thought you were
John Krasinski."

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:11.364
[ Laughter ]
I was like, "okay."

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.432
-At least he's honest.
I'll take that.

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:14.434
-"We thought you were famous.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:15.701
We thought you were John
Krasinski."

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:17.069
-I would take that.
Why not man?

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:18.705
-John Krasinski -- yeah.
-He's a stud.

00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:21.206
-Yeah, never walked the dog
again. That was it.

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:23.175
He wasn't welcome back.
-You're not John Krasinski,

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:25.945
yeah. I want to -- can we talk
about the show?

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.713
-Yeah.
-"Ordinary Joe."

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.715
I like the idea of this,
because --

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.418
the idea is kind of like,
to me,

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.620
like the movie "Sliding Doors."
Like, what if?

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.923
What if I made this decision
instead of this decision

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:39.926
in my life?
Would my life change?

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.027
Yes, of course it would,
but also the lives

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:43.963
around you would change as well.
-Yeah.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.699
-Is that kind of the idea?
-Yeah, so the show is,

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:49.268
basically, you meet the
character who I play,

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:50.670
his name is Joe Kimbrough.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.038
You meet him the day he's about
to graduate from college.

00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:55.975
And you see that he has three
very different choices

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.443
in front of him.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:00.680
And then the show cuts to black,
and it picks up 10 years later,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.148
and you follow the three
different

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:03.616
iterations of his life.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.585
So basically it plays with this
idea of choice,

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.553
and how the choices we make

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.355
drastically change
who we become.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:10.890
And in each world --
like, in one world

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.358
he's married with a kid.

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.260
In another world,
he remains single,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.963
and he became a nurse
at a hospital.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:19.098
In another world he became
like a famous musician

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.833
because he followed
his dreams of music,

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:23.536
and he has -- him and his wife
are trying to conceive,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.770
and they're having trouble
doing it.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.505
-So it was like -- this
is great for an actor.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:29.275
You must -- that's a meaty role.
-It's a challenge.

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.577
-You can do three -- you have to
do three different --

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:34.113
-Yes. --
-Characters in one episode.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:37.516
-Yes, we will -- sometimes we do
three characters in a day.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:39.718
Like, you're literally,
in the morning, you know,

00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:42.254
we'll do one character
and then two hours of, like,

00:03:42.254 --> 00:03:45.658
a hand-laid beard.
If you saw the show tonight,

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.060
he wears -- like, I have a long
hair and a beard

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.862
in one of the roles,
so we'll do that at lunch.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.030
-How fun.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.131
-Come back as a different
character. It's awesome.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.432
-And then you can take it
anywhere.

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:54.767
Who knows where the show can --
cause you go like,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.468
"Which one is real?"
-Right.

00:03:56.468 --> 00:03:58.604
-Could be the real --
I'm not writing for the show.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:00.673
[ Light laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.276
-Just saying, you go like, "Hey,
the rock star one is real."

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:06.045
Then you go, "No, it's not.
Just kidding."

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.613
-Well, do you have that --
do you have, like,

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.849
a moment like that where you
look back and you go like,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.284
"That was the -- that was the
crossroads where, like,

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:15.088
that drastically changed --"?
-Yeah, I think I did

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:16.388
reach that part.

00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:17.457
I think everyone has that thing
where you're like,

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:18.991
"I think I'm going to do this,"
or,

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:20.927
"I'm going to date this person,
or not this person."

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.094
-Totally.
-You make that move,

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:24.997
and it all can change.
I love it.

00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:26.199
-Yeah.
-I'm so excited about this.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.733
-Thanks, man.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:30.069
Here's James Wolk
in "Ordinary Joe."

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.638
Take a look at this.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:35.174
-Wow, you look fancy.
-Just a tie, bud.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.943
-Fancier than your scrubs.
-Yeah, a bit.

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:39.479
-You trying to impress people?

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.581
-Not really.

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:45.251
-Is mom going to be there?
-Yeah. It's her reunion too.

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:49.689
That's where we met, in college.
-I hope you impress her.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.762
-James Wolk, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:56.762 --> 00:05:00.766
Check out new episodes of
"Ordinary Joe" Monday nights

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:02.535
at 10:00 P.M. here on NBC.

